    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change: `namesakeCount` on owner create\n\nThe requirement: when an owner is created, return `namesakeCount` = the number of *existing* owners (before this create) sharing the same `firstName` and `lastName`, compared case-insensitively.\n\nI followed the existing pattern for on-create derived fields (`customerCode`, `householdId`), keeping each concern in its own small unit:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and it is documented on `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the persisted `namesake_count` column with a getter/setter, mirroring the other derived fields.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 marked `namesakeCount` as ignored when mapping from `OwnerFieldsDto` (it's a server-assigned field, exactly like `customerCode`/`householdId`); the reverse `toOwnerDto` maps it automatically.\n\n4. **`NamesakeCounter.java`** (new) \u2014 a single-responsibility component that counts, among a supplied collection of owners, those whose first and last name match a candidate case-insensitively. Kept pure (no repository dependency), mirroring `CustomerCodeGenerator`.\n\n5. **`ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner`** \u2014 for a *new* owner with no count yet, it fetches existing owners via the already-present `findByLastNameIgnoreCase` (reuse, no new repository methods across the three impls) and delegates the match/count to `NamesakeCounter`. The `isNew()` + `null` guards ensure it's computed once, at create, and never on update. Because the candidate isn't persisted yet, the query naturally excludes it, satisfying the \"before this create\" semantics.\n\n6. **Schema files** (h2/hsqldb/mysql/postgres) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column, consistent with how `household_id` etc. were rolled out.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreCountsNamesakes` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
